  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Algeria's footballing identity is inseparable from the complex, charged history of a nation that fought for its existence — and the 2026 World Cup drops them back onto the global stage with one of Africa's most technically gifted squads and a chip on their shoulder the size of the Sahara.",
    "footballIdentity": "Algerian football has long drawn from a unique dual identity — players born in France of Algerian descent choosing the green-and-white, creating a squad that bridges two football cultures. The 1982 'Disgrace of Gijón' — when Algeria were eliminated through a suspicious West Germany-Austria result — is still referenced as a foundational wound. The 2014 run to the last 16 and the 2019 AFCON title reminded the world that Les Fennecs belong at the top table.",
    "theMoment": "Algeria's relationship with France remains one of football's most politically charged fault lines — a colonial legacy that shapes the biographies of nearly every player in this squad. In 2026, many of these players hold dual French-Algerian nationality and have navigated immense pressure over their international allegiances. Every green jersey worn in North America carries that history into the arena.",
    "momentType": "political"
  },
